README:  AlphaCipher SDK

    This is the AlphaCipher SDK for Windows beta distribution.

RCS: @(#) $Id: $

Contents
--------

    1. Introduction
    2. Documentation
    3. Installing the SDK
    4. Testing the SDK

1. Introduction
---------------

The AlphaCipher SDK for Windows provides access to the "Navajo" one-time pad
(OTP) encryption engine kernel.  It complies with the ANSI C (ISO/IEC 9899) and
POSIX (IEEE 1003.1-2001) standards and includes a complete suite of specialized
unit tests to help ensure high build quality and performance.  The official SDK
binaries have been compiled and tested with the Visual C++ 2008 compiler and
runtime on the 32-bit Windows XP and Windows 7 operating systems.

2. Documentation
----------------

The public header files include overviews for each public function exposed by
the SDK.  Additional documentation is a work in progress.

3. Installing the SDK
---------------------

Prior to compiling and running SDK test application or unit tests, the binary
SDK components must be placed in a directory specified in the PATH environment
variable.  In addition, the SUBST command must be used to point the "X:\" drive
to the location of the SDK test keys by entering a command similar to the
following into a Command Prompt window:

    SUBST X: C:\full\path\to\keys

In the command above, the "C:\full\path\to\keys" argument must be the fully
qualified path of the extracted "keys" sub-directory.  In real applications,
this would not be necessary; however, the SDK test application assumes the SDK
test keys are located in the "X:\" directory.

4. Testing the SDK
------------------

The included SDK test application serves two purposes.  First, it can be used
to verify that the SDK components are operating properly.  Second, it serves as
a template for using the SDK components in other applications.

The SDK unit test suite uses the open-source Tcl-compatible scripting language
Eagle.  The included AlphaCipher Plugin for Eagle provides access to the SDK
components from Eagle scripts.  The plugin itself is not open-source; however,
the source code for the plugin has been provided under the same license terms
that govern the SDK components themselves.

The following steps are required to install and run the SDK unit test suite:

    1. Install the Eagle runtime by simply running the included self-extracting
       archive (SFX) file and selecting a suitable destination directory on the
       local machine.

    2. Install the AlphaCipher Plugin for Eagle by simply running the included
       self-extracting archive (SFX) and selecting the "lib" sub-directory of
       the Eagle installation directory chosen in the previous step.

    3. Open a Command Prompt window, change the current directory to the "bin"
       sub-directory of the Eagle installation directory chosen in step 1, and
       enter the following commands:

           SET PATH=%PATH%;C:\full\path\to\Win32_ReleaseDll\bin
           SET OtpKeyDir=X:\
           EagleShell -file ..\lib\AlphaCipher1.0\Tests\all.eagle

       In the first command above, the "C:\full\path\to\Win32_ReleaseDll\bin"
       portion of the first argument must be the fully qualified path of the
       extracted "bin" sub-directory containing both the "sqlite3.dll" and
       "ACKernel4Sdk.dll" files.

       In the second command above, the "X:\" portion of the first argument
       must be the fully qualified path of the extracted "keys" sub-directory.
       Alternatively, if the directions in the "Installing the SDK" section
       (above) have been followed (i.e. the SUBST command was executed), then
       the string "X:\" may be used verbatim (without the surrounding quotes).

       The SDK unit test suite will output and log full diagnostic information
       related to the configuration of the local machine, Eagle, the plugin,
       the SDK components, and the status of the tests themselves.  All the
       unit tests should either pass or be skipped (i.e. if the necessary test
       constraints are not met).  By default, the log file will be written to
       the directory specified by the TEMP or TMP environment variables.  This
       location, along with a wide variety of other Eagle test suite options,
       can be easily overriden via the command line; however, using the
       defaults is highly recommended.
